  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The evidence from the MyPathway study (pmid-29320312) supports the claim that the combination of Trastuzumab and Pertuzumab shows sensitivity/response in patients with Lung Non-small Cell Carcinoma (NSCLC) harboring ERBB2 (HER2) mutations. Specifically, among the 14 patients with HER2-mutated NSCLC who received the combination therapy, 3 patients (21%) achieved a partial response (PR) and 3 had stable disease (SD) lasting more than 120 days. This indicates clinical activity and response in this specific molecular and pathological context.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-29320312",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 402,
        "quote": "The phase 2a MyPathway study assigned patients with HER2, EGFR, BRAF or SHH alterations to treatment with pertuzumab plus trastuzumab, erlotinib, vemurafenib, or vismodegib, respectively. Among thirty-six patients that received treatment with trastuzumab plus pertuzumab because of a HER2 mutation (without amplification/overexpression) four patients (11%; 95% CI, 3% to 26%) had objective responses. \n"
      },
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-29320312",
        "start": 402,
        "end": 736,
        "quote": "Fourteen of the 36 patients with HER2-mutated tumors had NSCLC (adenocarcinoma, n = 13; adenosquamous, n = 1); in this group, three patients (21%; 95% CI, 5% to 51%) had PR and three had SD > 120 days. Only one of the other 22 patients with HER2-mutated tumors responded to treatment with pertuzumab plus trastuzumab (biliary cancer)."
      }
    ],
